<h2>The complaint and its background</h2>
<p>The wife's written complaint of 19 October 2020 led to Ekbalpore P.S. Case No. 365 of 2020 under Section 498A IPC against the husband, then about 69 years old. The gravamen was that he had concealed an extramarital relationship. The marital history was long and layered: a 1988 decree had declared the wife's earlier marriage void, the parties married in 1992, and by 2018 the wife had herself filed a suit for restitution of conjugal rights under Section 9 of the Hindu Marriage Act, alongside allegations concerning property transfers. A charge sheet followed on 31 December 2021, and cognizance was taken in January 2022. The husband sought quashing under Section 482 CrPC.</p>

<h2>Why the prosecution could not stand</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>No particulars</strong> — The FIR and Section 161 statements were general and omnibus, without dates, times or the manner of the alleged cruelty (paras 19-21).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Unpleaded improvements</strong> — Allegations of abuse appearing in the sons' police statements were absent from the mother's own written complaint (para 22).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Wrong statutory box</strong> — An extramarital relationship, without more, is not cruelty within the meaning of Section 498A (para 27).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Bhajan Lal applied</strong> — The case fell within categories 1, 3 and 7; continuation would be an abuse of process (paras 21, 29). The FIR and all proceedings were quashed qua the petitioner (paras 30-31).</div>
</div>

<p>Justice Ajay Kumar Gupta anchored the statutory point in the Supreme Court's decisions in <em>Pinakin Mahipatray Rawal v. State of Gujarat</em> and <em>Wasim v. State (NCT of Delhi)</em>: Section 498A cruelty means conduct likely to drive the woman to suicide or cause grave injury to life, limb or health, or harassment coupled with unlawful demands. Marital infidelity may wound, and it has civil and matrimonial consequences — but the Explanation to Section 498A, not the marriage's moral ledger, defines the crime.</p>

<h2>The dividing line</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Conduct</th><th>Where it is addressed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Extramarital relationship, without statutory cruelty</td><td>Divorce, judicial separation, maintenance consequences — civil forums</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conduct likely to drive the woman to suicide or cause grave injury</td><td>Section 498A IPC / Section 85 BNS — criminal prosecution</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harassment for dowry or unlawful demands</td><td>Section 498A Explanation (b), Dowry Prohibition Act</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domestic violence needing protection or residence orders</td><td>Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For accused spouses:</strong> where a 498A complaint pleads betrayal but no statutory cruelty — no particulars of acts, no demand, no injury — a quashing petition that maps each allegation against the Explanation to the section, and against the Bhajan Lal categories, can end the prosecution without a trial. The parallel matrimonial record matters too: pending civil suits often explain the complaint's timing and colour.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The decision does not hold that infidelity is beyond the criminal law in every case: where an affair is one strand in a pattern of statutory cruelty, prosecutions survive. What it refuses is the conversion of a broken marriage's bitterness into a criminal case unsupported by the particulars the offence requires. Under the BNS, the same analysis now applies to Section 85, which carries Section 498A forward.</p>
</div>
